ally  right I did my grandmother had a house  in a candy store in Queens my father was  a banker in Manhattan and we had a house  in the Hamptons as well right your first  job in the food industry was what  planing lettuce and tomato for burgers I  actually started washing dishes by hand  and that was my promotion was to put the  lettuce and tomatoes  so is my big promotion of the Sun  obviously did it well so how do you go  from doing that to getting sort of a  maybe more real job in the restaurant  industry you know I just I really was a  surfer in a skateboarder and I loved I  fell in love with the romance of the  restaurant business I worked in the  small pub in West Hampton Beach and  literally at the bar Jimmy Breslin  George Plimpton every soap pop you  noticed it was romance and excitement to  me so the romance was the people who  came in the atmosphere the conversations  you know the social stimulation I fell  in love with that that energy of the  restaurant business and luckily I had a  god-given talent to cook otherwise I  don't know what I'd be doing right now  you then move on obviously from there  and train and make your way eventually  to getting to Texas how did that happen  well you know I went to France cooking  school after the pub in West Hampton I  worked in a French restaurant that I  went to Paris I dropped out of school  and then I replaced Dean fairing at the  mansion on Turtle Creek which was an  amazing opportunity in position to take  and then I married a girl from West  Texas always following a girl we always  get you to come with us and then her  family was in the cattle ranching  business I started to learn about the  animal husbandry side of it and the  sourcing of Texas beef and that's how we  put together knife that along with going  to every Steakhouse in the country did  that change you in some way when you  when you when you got there and you and  you started that project you know I left  New York after 9/11 and I'd spent some  time in Lake Tahoe I came back to work  with Rick Moonen and then a headhunter  contacted me and said would you like to  replace Dean Ferenc and I was like it's  like one of those moments you take the  phone and pretend you're gonna like wait  and give an answer I was like I'm sure  I'll do that  yes but it was a competition I had to  cook off a bunch about I guess against a  hundred chefs to get the position Wow  and you know I fell in love with that  opportunity because it was a reinvention  of my career and it really changed my  life to replace Dean and we're still  close friends to this day is an amazing  gotta to change your life um it just  gave me an opportunity to refocus and to  be at the mansion to get back into fine  dining to get the hotel back to  five-star status it gave a lot of it yes  and it gave me a lot of attention on my  career and led to other opportunities so  and with the steak houses it's not just  a steakhouse the way we think of it  is it you know I was a seafood chef most  of my life but Texas is a meat and  potato kind of place so I was given the  opportunity of knife in the hotel with  the caveat that I had to do a steakhouse  so I literally went around to every  steakhouse in in the country to find out  what they had and didn't have to try to  reinvent it because I felt that Texas  needed that steakhouse like you know a  fish needs a bicycle so it's upright so  I really put my head to it and we found  sourcing and you know it's more of a  chef driven thing it's something you  can't really articulate you have to come  and experience it because it really is  about the flavor of the steak and the  product and the dry aging process that  we do well it is the dish so as you are  signing the dish yes ma'am you could  have a meal with anyone past or present  who would it be  you know just recently we were talking  about this in the green room I used to  work with Anthony Bourdain and such an  icon and we were friends at one time  we've been at odds do two magazine  articles about each other and comments  in books so I'd love to share a great  statement Anthony Bourdain might have  said something outrageous about you like  anime or the obverse but I would love to  sit around and have a couple of drinks  with him like we did back in the old  days yeah it'd be fun I'd be a fun meal  to actually be in on yes it would chef  John Key's our thank you so much thank  you very much and for more on John T's  our amma dish you can head to our  website at CBS this morning dot-com

